{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 40, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words or Less\n\nI landed in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms were peaking—pink confetti drifting over temple roofs and quiet alleys. No guidebook could prepare me for the stillness of Kinkaku-ji, the golden pavilion mirrored perfectly in its pond, untouched by tourists’ chatter. I sat on a bench, sipping matcha from a ceramic cup, feeling time slow.\n\nTokyo was a shock of light and sound—Neon signs flickered like electric rain, and Shibuya Crossing surged like a human tide. Yet in a tiny alley near Shinjuku, I found a 70-year-old ramen stall where the owner bowed before each bowl. The broth tasted like patience. I ate alone, watching salarymen and students share the same quiet reverence for food.\n\nI took the bullet train to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was hushed, the Atomic Bomb Dome standing like a broken hymn. At the museum, I read letters from children who never saw their 12th birthday. I cried quietly, then bought a paper crane from a survivor’s booth—folded by her own hands.\n\nIn Nara, deer bowed for snacks, their eyes knowing, ancient. I fed them shika senbei, and one pressed its forehead gently against my palm. No one told me to do it. I just felt it was right.\n\nEverywhere, there was beauty in restraint—a single maple leaf on a stone path, the way a shopkeeper bowed without words, the silence between train announcements. Japan doesn’t shout. It whispers, and if you listen, it teaches you how to be still.\n\nI came for the temples. I left with a quieter heart.\n\nThree weeks. One suitcase. A lifetime of small wonders.\n\n— Japan, 2023", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.002152984729036689}, {"toke